const DISERTACIONES = {
 "metodologia": {
  "tipo": "guia",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Methodology",
  "title": "How to write an essay",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>The philosophical essay <strong>is worth 3 points</strong> in the PAU (Option exercise, specific competences CE2, CE3, CE8 and CE9). It does not measure how much you know, but how you <strong>think</strong>: how you set out a problem, how you confront positions and how you defend your own. This guide sums up what it is, how it is structured and what rubric it is marked with.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>1. What an essay is</h2>\n<p>It is a <strong>personal text</strong> in which you clearly set out a <strong>philosophical problem</strong>, discuss the opposing positions that have been held throughout the history of philosophy, show that the question <strong>is still relevant today</strong> and express your <strong>own thinking</strong>.</p>\n<p>You are aiming for a clear, rigorous and coherent reflection that convinces the reader by means of <strong>logical argumentation</strong>. To achieve this, it is very useful to formulate <strong>provocative questions</strong> that challenge whoever reads and make them question their assumptions: after all, that is what Socrates did.</p>\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>‘To carry out philosophical work […] is to exercise thought, to master it and at the same time to change oneself […], the capacity of whoever reflects to settle in the heart of problems and live with them until the light dawns.’ — J. Russ, <em>Philosophical Methods</em> (2001)</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>2. What an essay is NOT</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>It is not an exercise in erudition</strong>: the point is not to set out many ideas from others, but to reflect with your own.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not a parade of knowledge</strong>: it stands out not for how many authors are cited, but for how their arguments are used (comparing them, analysing them, getting to the bottom of the question).</li>\n<li><strong>It is not a literary exercise</strong>: the aim is reflection, even though correctness, coherence and cohesion matter.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not a mathematical proof</strong>: there are no closed solutions; new questions often open up.</li>\n<li><strong>It is not a text commentary</strong>: you can start from a famous phrase, but what matters is what <strong>you</strong> contribute.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>3. Structure and length</h2>\n<p>The topic is presented as a question, a statement, a brief argument or a hypothetical situation. From it you must reflect on <strong>at least two positions</strong>, whether to consider both, to justify why you hold one, to propose a middle ground or to leave the question open. The most widespread model has <strong>three parts</strong>:</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What you do</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>0. Title</strong></td><td>Descriptive (it anticipates the central reflection) and, better still, <strong>suggestive</strong>: one that arouses interest.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>I. Introduction</strong></td><td>You present the problem and clearly set out the <strong>philosophical question</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>II. Development</strong></td><td>You set out the <strong>arguments for and against</strong>, drawing on at least two authors, and you <strong>relate it to the present</strong>.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>III. Conclusion</strong></td><td>You sum up and give your <strong>personal position</strong>, well argued, by way of closing.</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n<p>Approximate length: <strong>between 200 and 300 words</strong>. But worry about the <strong>content</strong> more than the length.</p>\n\n<h2>4. A guided example</h2>\n<p><strong>Question:</strong> what is more important today, convincing others or seeking the truth?</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>0. Title</strong> — e.g. ‘Likes or reasons’.</li>\n<li><strong>I. Introduction</strong> — we live in a society where communication, social media and politics depend greatly on the ability to convince. Is it worth more to persuade even when you are not right, or to strive to find the truth?</li>\n<li><strong>II. Development</strong> — <em>The Sophists</em> defended knowing how to argue and convince the assembly, even if truth was relative. <em>Socrates</em> believed that the essential thing was to seek the truth through dialogue, because knowing the good leads to doing good. And in the present: in politics, is rhetoric valued more than truth?; on social media, do likes matter more than arguments?; in your daily life, does looking good weigh more than being sincere?</li>\n<li><strong>III. Conclusion</strong> — do you prefer to be the one who ‘convinces’ or the one who ‘seeks the truth’? Philosophy teaches that it is not enough to speak well: you have to think well.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>5. PAU rubric</h2>\n<p>This is how it is marked (each competence with its range of points):</p>\n<table>\n<thead><tr><th>Competence</th><th>What it assesses</th><th>Points</th></tr></thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr><td><strong>CE2</strong> · Expression</td><td>Clear and precise language; formal register; good use of philosophical terminology.</td><td>0 – 0.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>CE3</strong> · Structure</td><td>Respects the structure (introduction, arguments for/against with connectors, personal opinion and closing conclusion).</td><td>0 – 0.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>CE9</strong> · Argumentation</td><td>Coherent, focused reflection; arguments from at least <strong>two relevant authors</strong>; well-argued personal opinion with examples; link with current affairs.</td><td>0 – 1.5</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>CE8</strong> · Originality</td><td>Suggestive title; quotation integrated with originality; provocative questions; own creation (no copying or use of AI).</td><td>0 – 0.5</td></tr>\n</tbody>\n</table>\n\n<h2>6. An example from the internet… to correct</h2>\n<p>The internet is full of ‘solved’ essays, but not all of them are good. Read the one from the 2025 Andalusia exam model published by teacher Rafael Robles, <strong>‘Is it possible to know the truth?’</strong>, and correct it with the rubric in hand.</p>\n<p><a href=\"https://www.rafaelrobles.com/2025/02/modelo-de-examen-de-selectividad-resuelto-pevau-andalucia-junio-de-2025/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Read the essay ‘Is it possible to know the truth?’ ↗</a></p>\n<ul>\n<li>What thesis does the writer defend? Where do they state it?</li>\n<li>They cite eight philosophers: do they argue with them or only summarise what they thought?</li>\n<li>Do all the ‘arguments’ answer the question? Look at Locke, Kant and Arendt.</li>\n<li>Does the conclusion follow from what came before? Is there a reasoned personal opinion? And a link with current affairs?</li>\n<li>How many words does it have? Would it fit in our test?</li>\n<li>A subtle clue: whose formula is <em>adaequatio rei et intellectus</em> really?</li>\n</ul>\n<details>\n<summary>See the answers</summary>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>There is no thesis.</strong> The writer never says what they defend: the introduction only announces that ‘it has been debated’.</li>\n<li><strong>It is a list of authors.</strong> It devotes one or two sentences to each philosopher without arguing with any of them. Writing more is not arguing better.</li>\n<li><strong>Arguments that do not answer.</strong> Locke explains where knowledge comes from, not whether it reaches the truth; Kant appears ‘in favour’, but what is said about him is a limit; Arendt talks about power manipulating the truth, which does not deny that it can be known.</li>\n<li><strong>Unearned conclusion.</strong> ‘Absolute knowledge seems unattainable’ does not follow from what came before. There is no reasoned personal opinion or current example.</li>\n<li><strong>Too long.</strong> About 450 words: almost double what our test asks for.</li>\n<li><strong>A mistake.</strong> The formula <em>adaequatio rei et intellectus</em> is scholastic (Thomas Aquinas); Aristotle has the idea, but not that formula.</li>\n</ul>\n</details>\n\n<h2>7. The same question, well answered</h2>\n<p>This is how it could turn out in under 300 words, with the structure of our test: introduction with the thesis, arguments in favour, arguments against and conclusion with an answer and personal opinion.</p>\n<p><strong>‘Leaving the cave’</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Introduction.</strong> Since Antiquity it has been debated whether the human being can gain access to the truth or whether, on the contrary, they only reach partial and subjective representations of reality. I will argue that we can know it: even if we only reach partial and revisable truths, merely wanting to seek them is enough to begin to be free.</p>\n<p><strong>In favour.</strong> Plato expressed it in the myth of the cave: the prisoners take the shadows for reality and do not even know they are in chains. Liberation hurts, and the ascent is gradual: whoever has only turned round has not yet seen the sun, but no longer mistakes the shadows for the things. Centuries later, Kant defined the Enlightenment as the exit from ‘immaturity’, the inability to use one’s own understanding without the guidance of another.</p>\n<p><strong>Against.</strong> The Sophists replied that there is no truth, only more or less persuasive opinions, and Nietzsche went further: behind the ‘will to truth’ a will to power would be hiding. Our world seems to prove them right: advertising, propaganda or a simple scam show daily that whoever persuades best wins, not whoever tells the truth.</p>\n<p><strong>Conclusion.</strong> However, that fear of being deceived, so well founded, proves that we distinguish deception from truth: deception produces real victims. And we do not need an absolute truth to escape it: between the false and a partial truth there is much more distance than between a partial truth and the whole one. That is why I believe the worst indifference is the one that disregards the harm that ignorance and deception cause to others. Although he would prefer to stay outside, the freed prisoner returns to the cave: knowing the truth is possible; sharing it, a duty.</p>"
 },
 "modelo-platon": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Plato",
  "title": "The just State (Plato)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p><strong>Question</strong>: ‘In Plato’s view, the just State is one in which each of the social classes fulfils the task that corresponds to it. Set out at least one argument in favour of this Platonic thesis and at least one objection; finally, set out your personal reflection.’ <em>(593 words · Block A)</em></p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Explanation of the thesis</h2>\n<p>According to Plato we must achieve a just State, and for this each social class is assigned a specific activity. That activity is suited to the <strong>proper capacity</strong> of the individuals who make it up: it is what they know how to do well that they must put into practice. In the ideal State each citizen will be happy because, thanks to their aptitudes, they devote themselves to the tasks they do best.</p>\n\n<h2>Arguments in favour</h2>\n<p>Fostering each person’s capacities is beneficial at both the personal and the state level, and it requires an <strong>educational process</strong>: Plato stresses time and again the importance of education. A system that promoted discovering and developing one’s own competences would be very positive. There are other valuable aspects: the division of labour, putting the general interest before the particular, and overcoming one’s own interests as a way of teaching <strong>solidarity</strong>. <strong>Cooperation</strong> is indispensable today for solving ecological or economic problems.</p>\n\n<h2>Objection</h2>\n<p>However, this ideal State cannot be achieved at any price, above all at the expense of what makes us persons: <strong>freedom</strong>. Plato restricts freedom and state intervention has no limits: private life is perfectly organised. Thus, the development of capacities would not be the complete development of the person, because their capacity to choose is limited. The State can <em>offer us</em> ways of being happy, but not <em>impose them</em>.</p>\n\n<h2>Personal reflection</h2>\n<p>I agree with Plato that individual happiness cannot be achieved at any cost: here the law must set insurmountable limits. But the moral education he seeks, although a good end, can only be achieved in freedom. In determining the common good we must avoid the danger of <strong>totalitarianism</strong>, and the way to do so is <strong>active democracy</strong> and civic education. Plato’s State is rigid and endangers individual development: the happiness of society prevails over that of the individual. That is why K. Popper, in <em>The Open Society and Its Enemies</em>, denounced Plato’s ideal State for not being democratic.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "modelo-kant": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Kant",
  "title": "Moral autonomy (Kant)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p><strong>Question</strong>: ‘In Kant’s view, the human being must use their own reason in building their moral conscience. This thesis is not shared by all the philosophers studied. Write a short essay with at least one idea in favour and one objection, and set out your personal reflection.’ <em>(Block C)</em></p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Explanation of the thesis</h2>\n<p>Kant considers that it is the subject themselves who must guide their action without subjecting reason to anything external. The moral law cannot be derived from elements superior to the individual, but from the <strong>autonomy of reason</strong>. That is why nothing—neither authority, nor tradition, nor the king, nor the priest, nor the teacher—has moral authority to impose imperatives on them: when that happens, the human being acts as if they were a <strong>minor</strong>. Each subject must impose the moral law on themselves: the imperative is not heteronomous but <strong>autonomous</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Arguments in favour</h2>\n<p>The most relevant argument is that ethics is grounded in the <strong>autonomy or freedom of the will</strong>, that is, in the capacity of reason to legislate for itself. Neither nature nor God imposes the moral law on us: it is the subject who gives it to themselves. Hence the Kantian distinction between <strong>morality</strong> and <strong>legality</strong>: obeying legal rules is not simply being moral, because there we follow an imperative imposed from outside and not the one that is born of conscience. Kant calls on each person to think for themselves: the Enlightenment is the exit from an immaturity caused by ‘laziness and cowardice’. Hence his motto: <strong>‘Sapere aude! Dare to think for yourself!’</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Objection</h2>\n<p>Now, the freedom that Kant proclaims is only <strong>individual</strong>. True to the Enlightenment, he does not perceive the influence of historical and social facts on people. <strong>Hegel</strong> showed that mentalities develop through custom over the course of history; <strong>Marx</strong> made it clear that social conditions determine real freedom: does an alienated society really allow one to be free? To the extent that the Kantian proposal is ideal, it is located outside reality, and its ethical propositions would reflect a <strong>bourgeois</strong> spirit in which only those who cover their material needs can enjoy that freedom.</p>\n\n<h2>Personal reflection</h2>\n<p>After the arguments for and against, I consider that each person must decide for themselves what suits them in order to develop their existence, taking into account the values accumulated in history (tradition, authority, society). Customs can be good, but it is also possible to go against them if a significant part of society considers it so in a given context (for example, #MeToo or #BlackLivesMatter). In Kant’s favour, freedom is—together with reason—a feature that defines the human being; but, with Hegel and above all Marx, it can be said that the human being is not only rational and free but a <strong>historical being</strong> who develops in history and shapes it. Kant’s transcendental subject is not entirely the subject we know in reality.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "modelo-nietzsche": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Nietzsche",
  "title": "God as an anti-life concept (Nietzsche)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p><strong>Question</strong>: ‘Write a short essay based on the thesis “the concept of God was invented as a counter-concept to life”. Present at least one idea in favour and one objection, and develop your personal reflection.’ <em>(792 words · Block C)</em></p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Explanation of the thesis</h2>\n<p>When Nietzsche speaks of ‘God’ he speaks of that which, for the individual, meant <strong>renouncing life</strong>. Proclaiming the existence of God means nothing other than the negation of the earthly life that belongs to each human being. Having God as the meaning of life turns the individual into a <strong>slave</strong>: values cease to refer to life—individual, bodily, changing, mortal—and refer to God instead.</p>\n\n<h2>Arguments in favour</h2>\n<p>The origin of the decadence of Western culture would be that negation of life produced by religion: in the name of a supposed future life, real life is excluded. Nietzsche (like Freud) accuses religion—and Christianity—of having rejected the passions, the drives and everything the body enjoys. That is why the <strong>‘death of God’</strong> opens the doors to the full development of the human being and to the liberation of their creative force. Since the fundamental reality is <strong>life</strong>, Nietzsche stresses its affective aspects and denies reason the capacity to grasp them. The destruction of current values imposes <strong>nihilism</strong>, which announces the overman and the <strong>revaluation of all values</strong>: moving from the morality of slaves (humility, sacrifice, compassion) to a morality that says ‘yes’ to life, after the three steps of the camel, the lion and the child.</p>\n\n<h2>Objection</h2>\n<p>This thinking carries risks: failing to control arrogance, violence or passion can have negative consequences for society and the individual, and we must not forget the <strong>links that some attributed to this mentality with Nazism and fascism</strong>.</p>\n\n<h2>Personal reflection</h2>\n<p>I think this thinking can have valuable aspects—for example, for a well-placed person, as a vital affirmation against conformism. But, being based on atheism and the exaltation of strength, it may not be the most suitable for someone who is ill, hungry or downcast. The affirmation of life should not turn into contempt for the weak.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "modelo-medieval": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Medieval",
  "title": "Can reason know God? (Scholasticism)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Model of a <strong>medieval essay</strong> following the scholastic method of the <em>quaestio disputata</em> (12th–14th centuries, University of Paris; Thomas Aquinas, Peter Abelard). It serves to see a <strong>different structure</strong> from the modern one: question → objections → <em>sed contra</em> → <em>respondeo</em> → replies.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Question</h2>\n<p>Is it possible for human reason to attain knowledge of God?</p>\n\n<h2>Objections</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Objection 1.</strong> It seems not: God is infinite and the human intellect is finite; the finite cannot encompass the infinite.</li>\n<li><strong>Objection 2.</strong> <em>Ecclesiastes</em> says: ‘no one can comprehend the works of God from beginning to end’. Therefore human knowledge cannot reach God.</li>\n<li><strong>Objection 3.</strong> All knowledge starts from the senses, but God is not sensible. Therefore reason cannot know him.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Sed contra</h2>\n<p><em>Romans</em> (1:20) says: ‘the invisible things of God can be seen through created things’. Therefore man can know God by natural reason.</p>\n\n<h2>Respondeo</h2>\n<p>I answer that the human intellect cannot know the divine <strong>essence</strong> in this life, because it exceeds every capacity of the creature. Nevertheless, it can <strong>know that God exists</strong> and some of his attributes—eternity, goodness, unity—from created effects, by way of causality, eminence and negation. As we know the walker by the footprint, so from creatures we arrive at an imperfect knowledge of the Creator.</p>\n\n<h2>Replies to the objections</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>To the 1st.</strong> We do not encompass the infinite, but we do know that it exists, as one knows that the sea is immense without taking it in whole.</li>\n<li><strong>To the 2nd.</strong> Scripture speaks of <em>total</em> comprehension, impossible in this life, but it does not deny partial knowledge.</li>\n<li><strong>To the 3rd.</strong> Although God is not sensible, sensible things are his effects, and through effects a causal knowledge of the cause is attained.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "modelo-descartes": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Descartes",
  "title": "Can reason ground everything? (Descartes)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p><strong>Question</strong>: ‘Can reason alone ground all knowledge? Write an essay. Give arguments for and against; to do so, use the different philosophical approaches that have existed throughout history. Finally, round off the essay with your personal opinion, an update to the present and a conclusion (200-300 words).’ <em>(Exercise 2 · PAU 2026, UPV/EHU)</em> · Linked reading: Descartes, <em>Discourse on Method</em>, Part IV.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>‘Foundations or habit: what does knowledge rest on?’</h2>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>After the crisis of the old authorities, modern philosophy asks whether absolutely certain knowledge is possible and who can give it a foundation: is reason enough, or is experience needed? The question is still alive: today we decide every day what to believe when screens offer us certainties à la carte.</p>\n\n<h3>First position: Descartes</h3>\n<p>Descartes says yes. In the <em>Discourse on Method</em> (1637) he doubts the senses, which sometimes deceive us, and reaches a first indubitable certainty: ‘I think, therefore I am’ (Part IV). From it he draws his criterion: only what we perceive clearly and distinctly is true. With innate ideas, deduction and mathematics as the model, reason rebuilds all knowledge from the subject.</p>\n\n<h3>Second position: Hume</h3>\n<p>Hume answers no: all knowledge comes from experience and there are no innate ideas. An idea without a prior impression is empty, and concepts such as causality or the self have no impression to back them: causality is an expectation born of custom and the self, a bundle of perceptions. Reason alone only grounds truths such as those of mathematics, which say nothing about matters of fact in the world.</p>\n\n<h3>Personal opinion and the present</h3>\n<p>I think Descartes is right to demand foundations, because without a criterion of truth anything goes; but his system pays a price: to get out of the self, it needs God as a guarantor. Hume better describes our ordinary life, although in return he leaves science itself without an absolute foundation. We see this today in research: a clinical trial is not decided by reasoning alone, but not without reasoning either, because data without method are just noise.</p>\n\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>My answer, then, is that reason alone cannot ground all knowledge; but experience alone cannot reach necessity either. We need both the Cartesian demand for evidence and the empiricist watchfulness of experience: thinking with method and checking with data.</p>\n\n<p><em><strong>Why it works.</strong> It answers the question and takes a position; two authors from the syllabus with their reasons (not just their names); a short, integrated quotation with work and part; a non-political update to the present; 288 words in the body (in the Spanish original).</em></p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "modelo-beauvoir": {
  "tipo": "modelo",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Model · Beauvoir",
  "title": "Is one born a woman, or does one become one? (Beauvoir)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p><strong>Question</strong>: ‘Is one born a woman, or does one become one? Write an essay. Give arguments for and against; to do so, use the different philosophical approaches that have existed throughout history. Finally, round off the essay with your personal opinion, an update to the present and a conclusion (200-300 words).’ <em>(Exercise 2 · PAU 2026, UPV/EHU)</em> · Linked reading: Beauvoir, <em>The Second Sex</em>, ‘Conclusion’.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>‘Nature or culture: is one born a woman, or does one become one?’</h2>\n<h3>Introduction</h3>\n<p>The question of woman seems old, but it is still open: is she defined by nature or constructed by culture? Behind it lies a bigger one: whether human beings have a fixed essence or make themselves in the situation they happen to live in.</p>\n\n<h3>First position: Aristotle</h3>\n<p>For Aristotle, every living being has an essence with an end of its own, and its good consists in realising it. Thus men and women would fulfil different natural functions, and society would merely recognise what nature has already laid down. It is an orderly and fruitful view: it explains the coherence of living things and fits a good part of our everyday experience.</p>\n\n<h3>Second position: Beauvoir</h3>\n<p>Beauvoir answers from existentialism: existence precedes essence and there is no given feminine nature. ‘One is not born, but rather becomes, a woman’ (<em>The Second Sex</em>, 1949, opening of volume II): the body is a fact, but its meaning is constructed by education, myths and institutions. Moreover, history has set up man as the Subject and woman as ‘the Other’, confined to tasks that serve other people’s projects. Femininity would therefore be more a matter of history than of destiny.</p>\n\n<h3>Personal opinion and the present</h3>\n<p>I think the debate does not force us to choose a single cause. The body matters, and biology is a fact; but societies interpret it in very different ways depending on the time and place, which suggests that there is construction too. Today people argue about toys, sport or ‘boys’ subjects’ and ‘girls’ subjects’: distinguishing the fact from its meaning helps us not to confuse nature with custom, or the other way round.</p>\n\n<h3>Conclusion</h3>\n<p>My answer is that one is born with a body, but that being a woman (its meaning, its roles, its expectations) is made within a historical situation that can be changed. Neither pure essence nor unconditioned freedom: a situated existence.</p>\n\n<p><em><strong>Why it works.</strong> It answers the question and takes a position; two authors from the syllabus with their reasons (classical essentialism presented fairly and the author’s existentialism); a short, integrated quotation with work and year; a descriptive update to the present, without an ideological verdict; 293 words in the body (in the Spanish original).</em></p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "comparativas": {
  "tipo": "comparativa",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Comparisons in pairs",
  "title": "Comparative models (in pairs)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>Six short models that <strong>pit two authors against each other</strong> on the same question—the usual exam format. Each one ends in ‘In my opinion…’: <strong>you write the personal conclusion</strong>. Use them as an argumentation template.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Marx ↔ Saint Augustine — what role does religion play?</h2>\n<p>For <strong>Marx</strong> religion is the ‘opium of the people’: it consoles in the face of the suffering and inequalities of capitalism by promising rewards in the afterlife, and thus keeps the masses passive and the established order in place. For <strong>Saint Augustine</strong>, by contrast, religion is a path towards God, absolute truth and true happiness. Today similar functions are served by consumerism, social media or mass sport. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<h2>Kant ↔ Saint Augustine — can the truth be known?</h2>\n<p>For <strong>Kant</strong> we only know scientific truth (a union of experience and reason) and only <strong>phenomena</strong>: the <em>noumenon</em> remains inaccessible (like the filtered image of a <em>YouTuber</em>, we see what is posted, not what is real). For <strong>Saint Augustine</strong> ultimate truth resides in God and is reached through faith and divine illumination, without the limits of human reason. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<h2>Kant ↔ Plato — the limits of knowledge</h2>\n<p><strong>Kant</strong> holds that we know scientific truth, limited by the conditions of our experience. <strong>Plato</strong> argues that true knowledge is <strong>recollection of the Forms</strong>, eternal realities that are reached through reason and not through the senses: the sensible world is an imperfect copy of the intelligible one. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<h2>Arendt ↔ Marx — what is the value of critical thinking?</h2>\n<p><strong>Arendt</strong>, in the Eichmann case, showed that not thinking from the other’s point of view leads to the <strong>banality of evil</strong>: critical thinking makes it possible to judge and to avoid blind obedience (today, in the face of hoaxes and manipulation). <strong>Marx</strong> warns that in an alienated society ideology guides thought and makes critical thinking almost impossible. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<h2>Arendt ↔ Saint Augustine — thinking for oneself</h2>\n<p>Against the need for critical thinking that <strong>Arendt</strong> defends, <strong>Saint Augustine</strong> holds that only God guides towards the truth: individual reflection detached from Him would lead to relativism and scepticism. Thus the <strong>autonomy of judgement</strong> and <strong>revealed truth</strong> are pitted against each other. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<h2>Saint Augustine ↔ Epicurus — is happiness possible?</h2>\n<p>For <strong>Saint Augustine</strong> true happiness is only attained in the encounter with God, the only imperishable good: the less attachment to the material, the happier. For <strong>Epicurus</strong> happiness is moderate pleasure and the absence of pain: freeing oneself from the fear of death and of the gods and satisfying only natural and necessary desires. <em>In my opinion…</em></p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "temas": {
  "tipo": "temas",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "Topics to practise",
  "title": "Dilemmas for essays (with authors from the syllabus)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>A <strong>bank of topics</strong> for practising the essay: present-day dilemmas that force you to think. For each one you have <strong>the question</strong>, the <strong>two positions in tension</strong>, some <strong>questions to get started</strong> and which <strong>authors from the syllabus</strong> to draw on. There is no correct answer: what is assessed is <strong>how you argue</strong> (remember the structure in the ‘How to write an essay’ guide). Inspired by the <em>arazo-egoerak</em> of Agora Filosofia Elkartea (topics adapted and rewritten).</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>1. Is inequality just?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Can a society with great differences in wealth be just, or does justice require redistribution?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> That each person should receive according to their function and merit, as against the idea that inequalities are only justified if they benefit the worst off; and the charge that inequality arises from economic exploitation.</p>\n<ul><li>Is equality before the law the same as real equality?</li><li>Does a ‘deserved’ inequality remain just if it condemns others to misery?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Plato</strong> (the just city), <strong>Rawls</strong> (the veil of ignorance) and <strong>Marx</strong> (exploitation and surplus value).</p>\n\n<h2>2. Can the State take a life? — the death penalty</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Is the death penalty permissible? Does it deter, or is it institutional revenge?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> <em>Retribution</em> and <em>deterrence</em> as against <strong>dignity</strong> as a limit: punishing a murder by killing repeats the evil it condemns, and an error is irreparable.</p>\n<ul><li>Is there evidence that it deters?</li><li>What about the risk of executing an innocent person?</li><li>Can the end (fewer crimes) justify any means?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Beccaria</strong> (Enlightenment: against cruel punishment), <strong>Kant</strong> (the person as an end, although he defends retribution) and the <strong>utilitarianism</strong> of Bentham and <strong>Mill</strong> (does it maximise the good?).</p>\n\n<h2>3. Is gender natural?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> What is expected of ‘a man’ or ‘a woman’: does it come from nature or is it built by culture?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> A <em>nature</em> that would fix roles, as against gender as a <strong>cultural and historical construction</strong>—‘one is not born a woman, one becomes one’.</p>\n<ul><li>Who benefits from presenting something as ‘natural’?</li><li>Can a custom be criticised as unjust even if it is very old?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Simone de Beauvoir</strong> (‘the Other’), <strong>feminism</strong> and the critique of patriarchal reason (e.g. Butler, performative gender).</p>\n\n<h2>4. Does scientific and technical progress have limits?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Should everything that science and technology can do be done? Is reason always liberating?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> The Enlightenment’s trust in progress, as against the warning that an <strong>instrumental reason</strong> (only means and ends) can turn into domination and catastrophe.</p>\n<ul><li>Can science tell us what we <em>ought</em> to do, or only what we <em>can</em> do?</li><li>What duties do we have to future generations and to the planet?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> the <strong>Frankfurt School</strong> (Adorno and Horkheimer: the dialectic of Enlightenment; Marcuse), as against the optimism of the <strong>Enlightenment</strong> (Kant); the principle of responsibility (Jonas).</p>\n\n<h2>5. Robots replacing workers?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> If machines do the work, is it a liberation or a sentence? Should an income be given to those who do not work?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> Technology as liberation from arduous work, as against the problem that, in a system where a wage gives access to life, automation leaves people out.</p>\n<ul><li>Does work define us? What is <em>alienated</em> work?</li><li>Who owns the benefit of the machine?</li><li>Is a basic income fair?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Marx</strong> (labour, alienation, surplus value) and the contemporary debate on distributive justice (<strong>Rawls</strong>).</p>\n\n<h2>6. Can we trust reason?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Is reason a safe guide towards truth and progress, or does it hide interests and illusions?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> The <strong>Enlightenment</strong> trusts in autonomous reason (‘dare to think’); the <strong>masters of suspicion</strong> show that behind our ‘truths’ there is power, economics or unconscious desire.</p>\n<ul><li>Do we think freely, or are we thought by culture, class or the unconscious?</li><li>Does suspecting everything lead to lucidity or to nihilism?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Kant</strong> (Enlightenment) as against <strong>Marx</strong> (ideology), <strong>Nietzsche</strong> (genealogy) and <strong>Freud</strong> (the unconscious).</p>\n\n<h2>7. Should freedom of expression have limits?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Should one be able to say (and laugh at) anything, or should some expressions be limited?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> Freedom also protects what <em>offends</em>—only <strong>harm</strong> to others would limit it—as against respect for dignity and the rejection of hate speech.</p>\n<ul><li>Is it the same to <strong>offend</strong> as to <strong>harm</strong>?</li><li>Who draws the line? Does censorship cure or make things worse?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>J. S. Mill</strong> (the harm principle, liberalism) and the debate on law and freedom.</p>\n\n<h2>8. Truth or post-truth?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Does truth exist and can we reach it, or does ‘everyone have their own’ and is everything interpretation?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> That there are no facts, only interpretations and perspectives, as against the idea that science and reason offer <em>criteria</em> for distinguishing the true from the false.</p>\n<ul><li>If everything is equally valid, why argue?</li><li>What does post-truth do to democracy?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Nietzsche</strong> (perspectivism) as against the philosophy of science (<strong>Popper</strong>, falsification; <strong>Kuhn</strong>, paradigms).</p>\n\n<h2>9. Surrogacy: free agreement or buying people?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> Should it be permitted to pay a woman to carry a baby for others?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> The <em>freedom</em> to decide about one’s own body and help others, as against the idea that it turns the body and a baby into a <strong>commodity</strong>, against the person as an <em>end</em> and not a means.</p>\n<ul><li>Can everything be bought and sold?</li><li>Does a ‘free’ contract make it fair if there is economic need involved?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Kant</strong> (dignity: an end in itself) and feminist critique; the liberalism of the contract as against its limits.</p>\n\n<h2>10. Should the State guarantee everyone what they need?</h2>\n<p><strong>The question.</strong> How far does the role of the State go: only protecting freedom, or also guaranteeing material conditions of life?</p>\n<p><strong>In tension.</strong> The minimal State that protects property and individual freedom, as against the State that corrects inequality and secures social rights.</p>\n<ul><li>Is someone free who lacks what they need to live?</li><li>Does freedom clash with equality, or do they need each other?</li></ul>\n<p><strong>Authors:</strong> <strong>Locke</strong> (liberal State, property) as against <strong>Marx</strong> (social critique) and <strong>Rawls</strong> (justice as fairness).</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 },
 "agora-2026": {
  "tipo": "temas",
  "subject": "hf",
  "kick": "ÁGORA 2026 proposal",
  "title": "The 14 ÁGORA questions (for and against)",
  "html": "<blockquote class=\"src\">\n<p>The <strong>fourteen essay questions</strong> of the <strong>Agora Filosofia Elkartea</strong> proposal for the PAU 2026, grouped into seven blocks. For each one you have <strong>arguments for and against</strong>, the <strong>authors on the syllabus</strong> to lean on and a <strong>trick case</strong>: the mistake that is easy to fall into. There is no right answer; what is assessed is <strong>how you argue</strong> (go over the guide ‘How to write an essay’). The questions are translated from Basque and freshly rewritten.</p> </blockquote>\n\n<h2>Block 1 · Being</h2>\n\n<h3>1. Do material and immaterial things exist?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour</strong> (yes, there are immaterial realities). There is something —the soul, the mind, the Forms, God— that is not bodily: <em>Plato</em> holds that the most real things are the Forms, eternal and immaterial, and that the sensible is only their copy; <em>Descartes</em> distinguishes two substances, <em>res cogitans</em> (thought) and <em>res extensa</em> (matter); <em>Augustine</em> places truth in a spiritual order.</p>\n<p><strong>Against</strong> (only the material exists). The ancient atomists (<em>Democritus</em>) already reduced everything to atoms and void; <em>Marx</em> maintains that what comes first are the material conditions and that ideas depend on them; for <em>Hume</em>, with no sense impression there is no idea with any content.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Plato, Descartes, Augustine, Marx, Hume. <strong>As extra support (not on the PAU syllabus):</strong> the atomism of <em>Democritus</em> (the Presocratics) and the monism of <em>Spinoza</em> (a single substance, <em>Deus sive Natura</em>).</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> confusing ‘immaterial’ with ‘unreal’ or ‘made up’. That something is not bodily (a number, a law of logic) does not mean it does not exist; and, conversely, to affirm that the immaterial exists does not oblige you to defend ghosts. Keep the metaphysical problem apart from the religious one.</p>\n\n<h3>2. Is the human being an open project or does it have an unchanging essence?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour of the open project.</strong> <em>Sartre</em>: ‘existence precedes essence’; first we exist and then we define ourselves by our acts. <em>Beauvoir</em>: ‘one is not born, but rather becomes, a woman’; what we are is built historically and culturally. <em>Ortega</em>: the human being has no nature, but a history.</p>\n<p><strong>In favour of the unchanging essence.</strong> <em>Plato</em> (the soul, immortal, defines what we are) and <em>Aristotle</em> (there is a human nature: the human being is a ‘rational animal’); the Christian tradition of <em>Augustine</em> (created in the image of God).</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Sartre and Beauvoir (existentialism), Ortega, over against Plato, Aristotle and Augustine.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> this is, almost word for word, a thesis that has already come up in the PAU 2026 (model II and the resit sitting). Be careful not to limit yourself to describing the existentialists: you have to <em>set</em> the two positions against each other and take a stand. And ‘open project’ is not ‘anything goes’: Sartre adds responsibility.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 2 · The good and justice</h2>\n\n<h3>3. Will whoever knows the idea of the good always act well?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour.</strong> <em>Socrates</em> and <em>Plato</em> defend <strong>moral intellectualism</strong>: no one does wrong knowingly, evil is ignorance; whoever truly knows the Good cannot will the opposite.</p>\n<p><strong>Against.</strong> <em>Aristotle</em> replies that knowing is not enough: there is <em>akrasia</em> (weakness of will), and virtue is acquired by habit, not by knowledge alone. <em>Hume</em> goes further: ‘reason is the slave of the passions’; what moves us to act is feeling. <em>Nietzsche</em> distrusts a morality that believes itself pure reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Socrates and Plato, over against Aristotle, Hume and Nietzsche.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> this too has come up almost word for word (the ordinary 2026 sitting). The typical mistake is to confuse ‘knowing the good’ with ‘knowing what the rule says’: we all know someone who knows what they should do and does not do it. That is precisely Aristotle’s argument.</p>\n\n<h3>4. Is there a universal good or justice, or do they depend on the point of view?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour of the universal.</strong> <em>Plato</em>: the Good is a Form that reason discovers, not invents; <em>Kant</em>: the moral law (the categorical imperative) holds for every rational being, without exceptions.</p>\n<p><strong>In favour of relativism.</strong> The <em>sophists</em> (<em>Protagoras</em>: ‘man is the measure of all things’) and <em>Nietzsche</em> (there are no moral facts, only interpretations and relations of power) hold that the good and the just change with culture and perspective.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Plato and Kant, over against the sophists and Nietzsche.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> relativism refutes itself if it is formulated as ‘everything is relative’ (that sentence claims to be a universal truth). But objectivism does not win either simply by saying that relativism is incoherent: you have to show <em>which</em> good is universal and <em>how</em> it is known.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 3 · Politics</h2>\n\n<h3>5. What is the most suitable form of government?</h3>\n<p><strong>One position: the government of knowledge.</strong> <em>Plato</em> defends the <strong>philosopher-king</strong>: the one who knows the Good should govern, not the majority; he distrusts democracy (which condemned Socrates).</p>\n<p><strong>Another position: the sovereignty of the people.</strong> <em>Rousseau</em> (the law must express the <em>general will</em>) and <em>Locke</em> (limited power, separation of powers, the right of resistance) found modern democracy. <em>Aristotle</em> proposes a middle way (the ‘politeia’, a mixture of democracy and oligarchy).</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Plato, over against Rousseau and Locke; Aristotle as the mean; the contractualism of Hobbes as a contrast (order in exchange for security).</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘democracy is the best because it is what we have today’ is not a philosophical argument, it is custom. You have to say <em>why</em>: protection of rights, control of power, political equality. And remember that for Plato the problem with democracy is demagoguery, not freedom.</p>\n\n<h3>6. What should the criterion of politics be: happiness or duty?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour of happiness (consequences).</strong> The <strong>utilitarianism</strong> of <em>Bentham</em> and <em>Mill</em>: a law or an action is good if it produces the greatest happiness for the greatest number; what matters is the results.</p>\n<p><strong>In favour of duty (principles).</strong> <em>Kant</em>: there are duties and rights that cannot be sacrificed even if general happiness increases; the person is an end in themselves, never a means.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Bentham and Mill (utilitarianism), over against Kant (the ethics of duty).</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> utilitarianism allows, in theory, the sacrifice of a minority ‘for the good of the majority’; that is its weak point and it is worth using. But Kantian duty has its own: what do we do when two duties clash? It is not enough to label each author; you have to put them to the test with a case.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 4 · Happiness</h2>\n\n<h3>7. Is happiness tied to knowledge?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour.</strong> <em>Socrates</em> and <em>Plato</em> (whoever knows the Good lives well); <em>Aristotle</em> (the highest happiness is the contemplative life, the activity of reason); <em>Augustine</em> (happiness is the possession of the Truth, which is God).</p>\n<p><strong>Against.</strong> <em>Epicurus</em>: happiness is <em>ataraxia</em>, the absence of pain and disturbance; it is achieved by moderating desires, not by piling up knowledge. <em>Nietzsche</em> and <em>Hume</em> stress the weight of the affections and of life over against pure reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Socrates, Plato, Aristotle and Augustine, over against Epicurus, Hume and Nietzsche.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> ‘knowledge’ is not the same as ‘having a lot of schooling’ or ‘being clever’. If you reduce it to that, the counter-example is immediate (very learned and very unhappy people). For Plato and Aristotle it is knowledge of the good or of the truth, not erudition.</p>\n\n<h3>8. Can the human being be happy?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour.</strong> <em>Aristotle</em> (<em>eudaimonia</em> is attainable through virtue and a life that goes well as a whole); <em>Epicurus</em> (yes, by freeing oneself from the fear of death and of the gods); <em>Augustine</em> (yes, in God).</p>\n<p><strong>Against, or with reservations.</strong> Life is marked by pain, loss and death; <em>Nietzsche</em> criticises the consoling recipes for happiness, and for the existentialists (<em>Sartre</em>) there is no guaranteed happiness, but freedom and responsibility in a world with no given meaning.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Aristotle, Epicurus, Augustine, over against Nietzsche and existentialism.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> it has come up almost word for word in the PAU 2026 (model I). The mistake is to turn it into a self-help essay (‘be happy with little’). It is a philosophical question: what happiness is (pleasure, virtue, meaning, salvation) determines whether or not it is attainable.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 5 · Faith and reason</h2>\n\n<h3>9. Can faith and reason be tools of the same level for knowledge?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour of harmony.</strong> <em>Augustine</em>: ‘I believe in order to understand’, faith and reason need each other; <em>Thomas Aquinas</em>: they are two distinct but compatible paths, and there are truths (such as the existence of God) that reason also reaches (the five ways).</p>\n<p><strong>Against (they are not of the same level).</strong> <em>Kant</em>: the existence of God cannot be proved theoretically, it is a <em>postulate</em> of practical reason; faith lies outside scientific knowledge. <em>Hume</em>: the arguments about God do not withstand empirical analysis.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Augustine and Thomas Aquinas, over against Kant and Hume.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> a delicate subject: treat it as an <strong>open debate</strong>, without saying whether God exists or whether faith ‘is true’. The question is epistemological (what each tool can know), not a religious stance.</p>\n\n<h3>10. Is faith a tool for manipulating reason?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour (the critique of suspicion).</strong> <em>Marx</em>: religion is the ‘opium of the people’, it consoles and keeps the unjust order in place; <em>Nietzsche</em> and <em>Freud</em> (religion as illusion or resentment) see in it a way of dominating or lulling to sleep.</p>\n<p><strong>Against.</strong> <em>Augustine</em> and <em>Thomas Aquinas</em> understand faith as illumination or openness to the truth, not as deception; the political use of a religion is one thing and faith itself is another.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Marx, Nietzsche and Freud (the masters of suspicion), over against Augustine and Thomas Aquinas.</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> <strong>an open debate, with no verdict.</strong> Distinguish two things that get mixed up: criticising the <em>use</em> of religion to manipulate is not the same as claiming that all faith is manipulation. Avoid the polemic for or against.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 6 · Truth and knowledge</h2>\n\n<h3>11. Is reason enough to find the truth?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour (rationalism).</strong> <em>Descartes</em>: reason, with methodical doubt and innate ideas, is the sure source (<em>cogito</em>); the senses deceive. <em>Plato</em>: the truth (the Forms) is reached by reason, not by the senses.</p>\n<p><strong>Against (empiricism).</strong> <em>Hume</em> and <em>Locke</em>: without experience there is no knowledge; reason alone spins in a void. <em>Kant</em> proposes the synthesis: there is no knowledge without experience or without the <em>a priori</em> structures of reason.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors from the syllabus:</strong> Descartes and Plato, against Hume and Locke; Kant as synthesis.</p>\n<p><strong>Trap case:</strong> do not fall into ‘reason for science and the senses for life’. The problem is one of foundation: where valid knowledge comes from. And beware: Kant does not say ‘a bit of each’, he says that experience and reason are inseparable.</p>\n\n<h3>12. Is objective knowledge possible, or is all knowledge subjective?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour of the objective.</strong> <em>Plato</em> (<em>episteme</em> as against mere <em>doxa</em>); <em>Kant</em> (we know the phenomenon, but with universal validity because we all share the same structures); science according to <em>Popper</em> (hypotheses that are corrected, but draw closer to the truth).</p>\n<p><strong>In favour of the subjective.</strong> <em>Protagoras</em> (each person is the measure); <em>Nietzsche</em> (perspectivism: there are only interpretations); <em>Kuhn</em> (science depends on historical paradigms).</p>\n<p><strong>Authors from the syllabus:</strong> Plato, Kant and Popper, against Protagoras, Nietzsche and Kuhn.</p>\n<p><strong>Trap case:</strong> connect this to today's <em>post-truth</em> without turning it into ‘everyone has their own truth’. If everything were subjective, there would be no point in arguing or in correcting a mistake; that is the limit of subjectivism.</p>\n\n<h2>Block 7 · Religions</h2>\n\n<h3>13. What is the function of religions? Is their effect positive or negative?</h3>\n<p><strong>Positive effect.</strong> For <em>Saint Augustine</em> and <em>Thomas Aquinas</em>, religion points towards truth and goodness, gives meaning and holds the community together.</p>\n<p><strong>Negative (or ambivalent) effect.</strong> <em>Marx</em> (it dulls class consciousness), <em>Nietzsche</em> (a morality that denies life) and <em>Freud</em> (an infantile illusion) stress its alienating effects.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors from the syllabus:</strong> Saint Augustine and Thomas Aquinas, against Marx, Nietzsche and Freud.</p>\n<p><strong>Trap case:</strong> <strong>open debate.</strong> Do not confuse ‘function’ with ‘truth’: one can analyse what religion is for in society without pronouncing on whether its beliefs are true. Avoid both apology and attack.</p>\n\n<h3>14. Has that function been replaced by something else?</h3>\n<p><strong>In favour (secularisation).</strong> In modern societies, functions that religion used to perform —giving meaning, holding people together, consoling— are now filled by consumerism, mass sport, social media, ideologies or trust in science and progress. This fits the thesis of <em>Nietzsche</em> (‘God is dead’: absolute values collapse) and the critique of <em>Marx</em>.</p>\n<p><strong>Against (the need persists).</strong> The question of meaning, death or evil does not disappear; for many thinkers no substitute covers it entirely, and religion is still alive.</p>\n<p><strong>Authors on the syllabus:</strong> Nietzsche and Marx (secularisation and critique), over against the persistence of the religious question (Augustine as a classic counterpoint).</p>\n<p><strong>Trick case:</strong> to ‘replace’ is not only to ‘appear in its place’; you have to show that the substitute fulfils <em>the same function</em> (giving meaning, consoling, uniting). To say ‘now people go to football instead of to Mass’ is an example, not an argument, as long as you do not explain what need it covers.</p>\n\n<blockquote class=\"src\">\n</blockquote>"
 }
};
